 :observations ["Checker parse-unparse.cljc parse-function splits the domain at ->/:->, passes every domain token to parse-cat, and parses the return, filters and object with unchanged opts. Its TODO explicitly proposes [arg :- type ...]. parse-object-path currently retains symbols or integers and warns for integers."
                "Runtime parse-ast.cljc parse-function groups each fixed-domain simple-symbol :- type triple before parsing types, counts grouped arguments for indices, and associates per-function object aliases; it parses body with incremented lexical depth. parse-object-path supports symbol/map syntax and computes nested lexical references; unknown symbols currently remain symbols and duplicates overwrite. This is evidence of grammar, not proof of checker execution or a mandate to replicate those permissive cases."
                "Checker distribute-regex-arities expands optional/regex domains; a source-token position is not necessarily a stable argument index after variable-width syntax. Checker object-rep Path IDs use NameRef; existing integer IDs are the established positional representation. abo/abstract-result operates on TCResult and can discard unmatched objects, so it must not be applied blindly to parsed Result."
                "The failed [coll :- x ...] candidate is grammatically evidenced by parse-ast but was rejected as a type by the checker; do not claim it already works. The existing seq RED tests cover a warning and behavioral examples but not independent binding equivalence."]
 :proposed-slice {:grammar "In a checker function domain, recognize the parse-ast fixed positional triple simple-symbol :- type, mixed with unnamed fixed types. Strip names before parse-cat, counting arguments rather than tokens. The seq replacement is [coll :- x :-> (t/SeqOn x) :object {:id coll :path [Seq]}]."
                  :type-scope "Keep type parsing and free/dotted type-variable environments unchanged; a term binder is not a type variable. Parse each domain type with original opts. Parse range and function result options with a separate, function-local term-object scope; do not add term names to type resolution."
                  :indices "Resolve a current-arity named object or proposition subject to its nonnegative fixed positional index without calling the integer-syntax warning. Preserve the original path and use existing Result and distribution machinery. Check both :then and :else filters and nested proposition forms through the same subject resolver."
                  :boundaries "Scope is per function arity, not shared between IFn alternatives. Reject duplicate names, malformed/incomplete triples, non-simple names, unknown references and references to a name from another arity within the new named-binder context. Nested functions must shadow cleanly; reject outer captures explicitly until depth-aware binding and substitution are proved. No blanket rejection of historical symbol IDs outside this new context."
                  :variable-domains "Allow names only on mandatory fixed prefix entries whose index cannot change. Preserve existing unnamed rest/dotted/keyword and regex handling after that prefix. Reject named rest/dotted/regex entries and named entries following any variable-width domain component; never erase, reinterpret, or silently rewrite unsupported syntax. Validate decisions against both actual grammars, especially parse-ast's raw-dom split, rather than claiming support for all checker regex forms."
                  :legacy "The integer source syntax remains accepted with its existing per-use deprecation warning, semantics and errors. Do not suppress warnings globally, alter legacy unnamed signatures, or change unrelated symbol handling. Preserve empty/no-object behavior."
                  :risk "Unknown-vs-legacy symbol discrimination and nested type traversal need precise scope markers: do not extend named-mode rejection to independent nested legacy annotations accidentally. If the source cannot distinguish an intentional free symbol from an unknown named reference without changing existing contracts, hold implementation for design adjudication rather than break compatibility. Likewise do not assert nested lexical support merely because parse-ast constructs Lexical ASTs."}
